23:28:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a brilliant observation, and the answer lies in a whole string of differences in scale, technology, and business model. Let’s break it down. --- ### 1. The Ink Is Completely Different (and Much Cheaper) Your home printer uses **liquid inkjet ink** or **toner**—precision chemicals designed to work in tiny nozzles and deliver photo-quality results on a wide range of paper. It’s packed in small cartridges with costly microchips, packaging, and distribution chains. The cost per milliliter is astronomical. Newspapers, on the other hand, use **coldset web offset printing ink**, which is essentially a cheap, thick paste made from carbon black (or colored pigments) suspended in oil or soy-based vehicles. They buy it by the tanker-load or in huge 200-kg barrels. The cost per kilogram can be a fraction of consumer ink. These inks dry by absorption into newsprint, not by evaporation, so they’re simpler and cheaper. --- ### 2. Economies of Scale: Bulk Purchasing When you buy a tiny ink cartridge at a retail store, you’re paying for: - The cartridge itself (plastic, metal, chips) - Packaging, shipping, shelf space, retail margin - R&D and marketing A newspaper company buys **raw ink directly from manufacturers** in massive quantities, often under long-term contracts. The price per unit plummets. They’re not paying for thousands of tiny cartridges—they’re pumping ink from silos directly into the presses. --- ### 3. Industrial Printing Speed and Efficiency Home printers are stop-start, small-format devices that clean printheads, perform alignments, and waste ink during each cycle. The cost per page is high because a lot of effort goes into keeping the machine ready for one-off prints. Newspaper presses are **web-fed rotary giants**: continuous rolls of paper run at speeds exceeding 80 km/h (50 mph) through cylinders that transfer ink in exact amounts. The process is so efficient that ink is applied at fractions of a cent per page. The fixed costs (press, maintenance, staff) are enormous, but the variable cost **per copy** is astonishingly low once you’re printing hundreds of thousands. --- ### 4. The Paper Is Cheaper Too You might be tempted to use nice white A4 paper, which is more expensive. Newsprint is a low-grade, uncoated, thin paper made mostly from recycled fiber or mechanical pulp. It’s produced in vast widths and wound into gigantic rolls. The paper itself costs the publisher only a few cents per copy. --- ### 5. The Business Model: Cover Price ≠ Profit Many newspapers sell at a price that **barely covers the printing and distribution cost**—sometimes even less. The real money comes from **advertising**. Advertisers pay for the audience, subsidizing the whole operation. Your ink cartridge, however, is sold by a company that makes its profit directly from **you** on that cartridge (often following the razor-and-blades model: cheap printer, expensive ink). So you’re paying a high margin on a product whose only source of revenue is the consumable itself. --- ### A Quick Cost Comparison (Approximate) - **Home inkjet:** ~$0.10–$0.30+ per page for color (ink alone), depending on coverage. - **Newspaper web offset:** ink cost per page can be as low as **$0.0001 to $0.0005** (one hundredth of a cent) for typical text and images. So, your 20x estimate might actually be quite conservative — it can be 200x–2000x more expensive for you to print the same amount of coverage at home, mostly because you’re using a precision chemical in a tiny package versus a tanker of oily paste. --- In short, it’s the difference between buying a thimble of artisanal ink at a boutique and buying a swimming pool’s worth of industrial-grade ink at factory-gate price, while running machines that never stop and having advertisers pick up the tab.
